  <h2 id="what">What kdev is</h2>
  <p>
    kdev is a command-line tool, released under the <a href={links.license}>MIT licence</a>, that uses Kaggle's public API and Cloudflare Tunnel to run a
    Kaggle notebook session as a remote development machine. Its source is public at <a href={site.repo}>github.com/tushar-mahalya/kdev</a>. It
    was built to study and document how notebook sessions, saved versions and outbound tunnels behave, and to teach the techniques involved.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-responsibility">Your responsibility</h2>
  <p>You alone are responsible for how you use kdev and for the accounts you use it with. Before you run it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Read and follow Kaggle's Terms of Use and community guidelines.</strong> Kaggle allows one account per person and restricts
      tunnelling out of notebooks. Accounts have been banned for both. kdev does nothing to hide what it does, and it cannot make a use acceptable
      that the terms forbid.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Read and follow Cloudflare's terms</strong> for any account, domain or tunnel you connect.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use only accounts you are entitled to use.</strong> Pooling quota across a Kaggle group means accounts that belong to real, distinct
      people who have each agreed to it. Do not create or borrow accounts to get more compute.
    </li>
    <li>Do not use kdev to break any law, to attack or overload any service, or to get around any limit, quota or security control.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you are not sure that a use is allowed, don't run it until you are.</p>

